WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.309
A cache is there to protect the database.

2
00:00:05.309 --> 00:00:07.791
Every answer it gives is a query saved.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.860
So can a cache ever make the database busier?

4
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.755
At ten one morning, the database gets the same query a hundred times at once.

5
00:00:15.755 --> 00:00:17.535
And the cache is the reason.

6
00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:21.101
Picture a bakery with a shelf of bread by the door.

7
00:00:21.101 --> 00:00:23.016
Customers take a loaf and go.

8
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:29.321
When the shelf is empty, a customer asks the baker at the back, who takes a while to bake one.

9
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.400
In the payments app, the shelf is Redis, a program that keeps data in memory.

10
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.217
It holds copies of rows from the slower database, the baker.

11
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.980
A loaf is one copy of a row, with a sell-by time: its TTL, or time to live.

12
00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.584
When it runs out, the copy is gone.

13
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:48.534
The rule: ask Redis first.

14
00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:50.912
If the copy is there, that's a hit.

15
00:00:50.912 --> 00:00:56.621
If not, that's a miss: load the row from the database, put it back in Redis, and go.

16
00:00:56.833 --> 00:01:03.027
Now take the busiest shop on a sale day, merchant m-17, read a hundred times a second.

17
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:07.531
Every read is a hit, and the database never hears about it.

18
00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:10.631
Then, at ten o'clock, the TTL runs out.

19
00:01:10.631 --> 00:01:13.827
And loading the row takes 200 milliseconds.

20
00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:19.554
Within a few milliseconds, a hundred requests ask for the key, and all of them miss.

21
00:01:19.554 --> 00:01:21.262
Each one follows the rule.

22
00:01:21.262 --> 00:01:22.971
How many database queries?

23
00:01:22.971 --> 00:01:24.023
Pause and guess.

24
00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:32.912
The hopeful answer is one: the first request reloads it, and the rest find it in Redis.

25
00:01:32.912 --> 00:01:37.743
But the reload takes 200 milliseconds, and all hundred have already asked.

26
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:43.572
None of them knows about the others, so each one queries the database: a hundred queries.

27
00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:50.941
With a pool of 20 database connections, 80 queries wait in line, and every other query waits behind them.

28
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:59.256
This is a cache stampede, also called a thundering herd: a hundred customers walking to the back of the bakery for the same loaf.

29
00:01:59.467 --> 00:02:02.460
The baker bakes the same bread a hundred times.

30
00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.261
Very fresh, and ninety nine times pointless.

31
00:02:05.633 --> 00:02:10.099
So the moment a busy copy expires, the cache sends everyone at once.

32
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.029
What we want is what polite customers do.

33
00:02:13.029 --> 00:02:15.824
The first one says: I've asked, wait here.

34
00:02:15.824 --> 00:02:17.488
Let's replay ten o'clock.

35
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:22.522
Inside one program, each request runs as a thread, one path of work.

36
00:02:22.522 --> 00:02:28.337
The threads share a map, a table of keys and values: in Java, a ConcurrentHashMap.

37
00:02:28.533 --> 00:02:39.420
Each thread that misses makes an empty box for the result, a CompletableFuture, and tries putIfAbsent: add my box under the shop's key, only if nothing is there yet.

38
00:02:39.633 --> 00:02:41.232
The first thread succeeds.

39
00:02:41.232 --> 00:02:43.999
It's the leader, and it queries the database.

40
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.729
The other 99 find the leader's box already there, and wait on it.

41
00:02:48.933 --> 00:02:54.039
After 200 milliseconds, the leader puts the row in Redis and in its box.

42
00:02:54.233 --> 00:02:58.805
All 99 wake up with the same value, and the leader clears its entry.

43
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:04.243
One query instead of a hundred, and everyone waited about 210 milliseconds.

44
00:03:04.243 --> 00:03:07.739
This is single-flight: one load in flight per key.

45
00:03:07.933 --> 00:03:12.228
A hundred customers, one loaf, and a baker who gets to sit down.

46
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.640
So far so good.

47
00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:20.016
But a busy app runs many copies of the same program, so one can fail and the work is shared.

48
00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:24.499
Say four copies, 25 requests each, each with its own map.

49
00:03:24.499 --> 00:03:26.071
How many queries now?

50
00:03:26.633 --> 00:03:27.006
Four.

51
00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:33.047
Each copy's leader asks the database, because no copy can see another copy's map.

52
00:03:33.233 --> 00:03:35.090
Here the bakery picture breaks.

53
00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:37.845
In a shop, everyone sees who's at the counter.

54
00:03:37.845 --> 00:03:42.576
Copies of an app see nothing of each other: they need a sign on a shared board.

55
00:03:42.733 --> 00:03:46.309
Redis can be that board, since every copy talks to it.

56
00:03:46.309 --> 00:03:48.229
Replay ten o'clock once more.

57
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:57.132
On a miss, the copy tries to put up the sign: SET lock:merchant:m-17, a token, NX, PX 2000.

58
00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:02.191
NX means only if the key doesn't exist yet, so exactly one copy wins.

59
00:04:02.191 --> 00:04:04.460
The token says whose sign it is.

60
00:04:04.633 --> 00:04:11.659
PX 2000 rubs the sign out after 2,000 milliseconds, in case its writer wanders off.

61
00:04:12.033 --> 00:04:14.859
The winner loads the row and puts it in Redis.

62
00:04:14.859 --> 00:04:18.852
The others check Redis every 10 milliseconds, for up to a second.

63
00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:23.553
In the lesson's demo, that made one database call across four copies.

64
00:04:24.033 --> 00:04:25.614
This lock only saves work.

65
00:04:25.614 --> 00:04:32.665
If the winner crashes before it fills the cache, the others stop waiting after a second and load the row themselves.

66
00:04:32.833 --> 00:04:35.311
That's wasted work, not a wrong answer.

67
00:04:35.311 --> 00:04:39.631
A lock that must never fail is a harder problem, for a later lesson.

68
00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:45.024
And keep single-flight too, so only one thread per copy even reaches for the sign.

69
00:04:45.367 --> 00:04:48.531
Here are both pieces, in Java and in Kotlin.

70
00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:53.839
putIfAbsent picks one leader inside a copy, and everyone else joins its future.

71
00:04:54.067 --> 00:04:58.267
SET with NX and PX picks one copy across all of them.

72
00:04:58.267 --> 00:05:01.675
Both languages send Redis the same command.

73
00:05:02.267 --> 00:05:05.037
So, can a cache make the database busier?

74
00:05:05.037 --> 00:05:09.768
For a moment, yes: when a busy copy expires, everyone misses together.

75
00:05:09.967 --> 00:05:17.739
The fix is to send one customer to the back: single-flight inside each copy, and a sign in Redis across all of them.

76
00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.257
One quick check.

77
00:05:19.257 --> 00:05:23.668
Twenty copies, each with single-flight, and no sign in Redis.

78
00:05:23.668 --> 00:05:25.187
The busy key expires.

79
00:05:25.187 --> 00:05:27.067
How many database queries?

80
00:05:27.067 --> 00:05:28.224
Pause and think.

81
00:05:31.433 --> 00:05:36.240
Twenty: one leader in each copy, because no copy can see another's map.

82
00:05:36.433 --> 00:05:40.088
Next, we'll bake the next loaf a little before the shelf runs out.
